1. Can a wiki tree make inferences?

Yes — but not the way most people imagine.

The common mistake is to think the world model needs to hold all knowledge. It doesn't. It needs to hold the shape of knowledge, and fetch the content on demand.

Here's the distinction:

Encyclopedic model (impossible):

· Hold every fact about every place, person, and thing.
· 10 million entries. Gigabytes of data. Stale within months.

Relational model (feasible):

· Hold the relationships between categories.
· The tree knows: France is_a country, is_in Europe, has_attribute population, has_attribute GDP.
· It doesn't know the values. When you ask "GDP per capita of France?", it says: "I need GDP. I need population. Fetching."
· Wikipedia/Wikidata provides the values. The Calculator divides them. The answer emerges.

That's the key insight. The council's World Model doesn't need to know everything. It needs to know what to fetch, and what to do with it. The tree provides the skeleton. The network provides the flesh. The Calculator and Causal Engine provide the reasoning.

This is how real expert systems work. MYCIN didn't know every disease. It knew the relationships between symptoms, causes, and treatments — and asked questions until it could narrow down the answer.

